with d as (select value r from jsonb_array_elements($q$[{"id":"7b283118-9d36-40b4-b3e2-8fe3fcf54128","notes":["Wild fennel (fronda di finocchietto) is the leafy fronds, not the seeds. In Australia, you may find it frozen in the Asian or Italian section; if not, a small amount of fresh dill mixed with a pinch of ground fennel seed is an acceptable substitute, though the taste won't be quite the same.","The pasta can be made up to 2 hours ahead and kept covered on the bench. You can also freeze it on a tray, then bag it; cook from frozen, adding 1–2 minutes to the cooking time.","If fresh sardines are hard to find, use tinned sardines in oil (drain well and add them to the sauce at the end, just to warm through, so they don't fall apart).","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"0bdf6c65-9c69-469a-9959-8c705b700435","notes":["The key is steam. Wrap that lid in a towel, no shortcuts. If your kitchen is very cold, you may need another 5 minutes.","Listen for the gentle crackling around 15 minutes in. That's how you know it's working.","If the tahdig is too dark or burns, it happens. Serve it anyway, bitter tahdig with yoghurt is still tahdig."]},{"id":"0355182e-0165-4aa7-8187-6a28533cf282","notes":["Cook the shrimp in batches to avoid crowding the pan, this ensures even cooking and a proper sear.","Stone-ground grits take longer than instant grits and have better flavour. Don't skip the low-and-slow cooking.","Food safety: Cook bacon and cured pork until the fat is rendered and the meat is hot right through. Cook prawns and other crustaceans until the shells turn pink and the flesh is opaque and firm."]},{"id":"f23e468e-0cd4-4fc1-b1ce-2bcb33fe91e5","notes":["Make it ahead: prep the vegetables and pat the chicken dry the night before, then assemble and roast when you get home.","Swap in whatever root veg you have, parsnips, sweet potato or beets all work. Keep the total weight around 900 g.","Leftovers reheat brilliantly at 180°C for 15 minutes, or shred the chicken and toss with the vegetables for a next-day grain bowl.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"1c3c4a2a-b4d7-41be-b7b2-e8625fc8551c","notes":["If fresh crawfish isn't available, frozen works just fine, thaw it first.","The roux is everything: low and slow, stirring often. Once it's dark brown, you've got the soul of the dish.","Make this ahead: the étouffée keeps for 3 days in the fridge and tastes even better reheated.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."]},{"id":"c3cd451e-ca20-43ad-a93a-88a10f72319e","notes":["Żur (sour rye starter) can be found in Polish or Eastern European grocery shops, or made at home by fermenting rye flour in water for 24 hours. It keeps in the fridge for weeks.","Make this a day ahead if you can, the flavours deepen overnight. Reheat gently on the stove, adding a splash of water if needed.","Good kielbasa makes this soup. Don't skip it or use thin sausages; you need that smoky, meaty backbone.","Cook mince and sausages until they reach 75°C in the centre, with no pink left."]},{"id":"7a2e12ed-07a6-447d-92b9-cffb8892e67f","notes":["Make it a day ahead: the flavours deepen as it sits. Reheat gently on the stove top.","Serve with rice and peas, or plain white rice to soak up the curry.","Can't find goat? Lamb shoulder works, though it cooks faster, check it at 60 minutes."]},{"id":"28a653d3-8eb2-48da-8c11-395c8d9fda18","notes":["Marinade the paneer for at least 10 minutes, but up to 2 hours in the fridge makes it taste even better.","Use a good non-stick pan or oil-brushed stainless steel so the paneer doesn't stick. Don't move the pieces around too much, let them get a nice golden crust.","If you have wooden skewers, soak them in water for 30 minutes before threading."]},{"id":"457aecb1-2b00-49aa-93a6-7f201bb99f43","notes":["Keeps in an airtight container in the fridge for 3 days.","Make ahead: prepare and slice the day before; store in an airtight container and eat chilled or bring to room temperature.","For extra fillings, try adding finely chopped mushroom or zucchini, just squeeze out excess moisture first."]},{"id":"52cb54e1-a8fa-4e24-9723-c7ec93ad9603","notes":["Freeze the cooked mince mixture in portions for an even quicker future dinner, just thaw and warm through.","Swap flour tortillas for corn ones, or lettuce leaves if you're out of bread.","Greek yoghurt works just as well as sour cream here.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"a9821d09-3604-4359-b3da-9fba6595a026","notes":["Make the pastry up to 2 days ahead and store in the fridge; the filling can also be made a day early and reheated gently before assembling.","If you can't find mixed mushrooms, use whatever is available, button mushrooms alone work just fine, though you'll lose some of that forest-floor flavour.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"de8d653a-6137-4b5a-9e82-f5fe3c690b63","notes":["Make it ahead: tabbouleh keeps in the fridge for up to 2 days in an airtight container. The flavours actually get better the next day.","Swap the fresh mint for dried mint if that's all you have, use about a third of the amount.","Serve with cos lettuce leaves or pita bread for scooping."]},{"id":"97683ac5-fe7f-4354-8989-26344dfcfb85","notes":["Make ahead: prepare the dalo and taro leaves the day before, stored in separate airtight containers in the fridge.","Swap the white fish for any firm fish, use what's fresh. Trevally, snapper or bream all work beautifully.","Use coconut cream mixed with a little water if you can't find coconut milk, or stir in 2 tbsp grated fresh coconut at the end for deeper flavour.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."]},{"id":"236cf3c7-5fed-4d9b-855d-7389189ddaec","notes":["If you can't find fresh lemon myrtle leaves, dried lemon myrtle powder works well, use about 1 tsp mixed with a little salt and pepper to season the cavity and skin.","The pan juices make a simple sauce: deglaze the hot tray with a splash of water or stock, scraping up the browned bits, and drizzle over the carved chicken.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"cfb955bd-0d5d-4811-851b-d757fd07eb67","notes":["Gudeg keeps well in the fridge for up to 3 days and tastes even better the next day as the flavours deepen. Reheat gently on the stove.","Serve with steamed rice and a simple fried chicken or hard-boiled eggs on the side, Yogyakarta style."]},{"id":"a691d514-ba4a-45d7-b39e-1bee77cd0117","notes":["The marinade works best overnight. Plan ahead or marinate for at least 4 hours.","Leftover carne asada is great in tacos, quesadillas or over rice the next day.","Food safety: Cook whole cuts of beef, lamb and pork to at least 63°C in the thickest part, then rest for 3 minutes; cook pork and any rolled or stuffed cuts until the juices run clear."]},{"id":"897abfbf-dee4-43a9-a760-ceb02f369527","notes":["This freezes beautifully, make a double batch and freeze half. Thaw overnight and reheat gently.","Any root vegetables work here: turnip instead of swede, celeriac, or even a bit of cabbage. Swap parsnip for more carrot if that's what you've got.","If you can't find a ham bone, a ham hock works just as well, or use chicken stock and add a splash of ham or bacon for flavour."]},{"id":"78d72b9b-bccb-4c0b-8111-11230c7eb742","notes":["Marinate overnight if you have the time, it'll be even more tender.","Cook over charcoal if you can. It makes all the difference, mate.","Pink lamb is the way. It's not overcooked until it's grey.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"7a88a94c-c215-446d-b7f0-9764bec7d5cc","notes":["Make the broth the night before and chill it; the fat will solidify on top and you can lift it off. This also lets the flavours develop further overnight.","If chicken bones are hard to find, ask the butcher, they'll usually give them to you cheap or free. Chicken feet add real gelatin and depth to the broth.","You can poach a whole chicken breast in the simmering broth for the last 20 minutes, then shred it to serve. Or buy a rotisserie chicken and shred that instead.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"4adb4a54-22c8-439e-9cc6-ed0e63b9a52e","notes":["Make ahead: prepare the filling and dough the day before, keep both covered in the fridge. Assemble and bake fresh when you're ready.","Freeze: uncooked empanadas freeze well for up to a month. Bake straight from frozen, adding 5–7 minutes to the cooking time.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"acb94b1d-73d1-464f-8bfd-ee8e0974c2c5","notes":["Use only the freshest, sashimi-grade ahi from a reliable fishmonger. Serve immediately after assembly.","Make the rice ahead and chill it completely before building the bowls.","Swap cucumber for daikon radish if you want a bit of crunch and bite.","The fish here is eaten raw or only cured in citrus. Buy sashimi-grade fish from a fishmonger, keep it cold and eat it the same day; skip it for anyone pregnant, very young, elderly or with low immunity.","Food safety: The seafood here is served raw or cured, so buy sashimi-grade, keep it cold and eat it the day it is made; for anyone who should avoid raw fish, cook it until opaque. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"7244a8f2-725e-4613-9ebc-d01a7c214eae","notes":["The key is bringing the fish to room temperature first, it cooks through more evenly. If your perch is larger (750 g), add 2–3 minutes to the steaming time.","If you can't find perch, use any firm white fish whole, about 500 g: bream, snapper, or barramundi work well.","Sashimi-grade fish is not needed here; a regular market-fresh white fish is perfect."]},{"id":"d05a6711-289d-431f-892a-5d8286371d65","notes":["Serve hot for a traditional bowl, or chill it and serve cold on a warm day.","The balance of tamarind and sugar is personal, start with less sugar and add to taste as you simmer."]},{"id":"54c76c97-af1e-4c1c-a1a7-46fabf958d83","notes":["This tastes better the next day, make it ahead and reheat gently.","If pearl onions are hard to find, use small shallots or just brown onion cut into chunks; the cooking time stays the same.","Serve with crusty bread, rice, or soft polenta to soak up the incredible sauce.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"1320fb7f-5dd0-45dc-b17d-d07af64429ea","notes":["Slice the steak against the grain, it'll be more tender. If it's too thick, pop it in the freezer for 15 minutes first to make it easier to slice.","Any supermarket steak works: the thinner you slice it, the less time it needs. Rib eye stays juicy, sirloin is leaner.","Make the marinade ahead and marinate the beef for up to 2 hours if you want deeper flavour, just let it come to room temperature before cooking.","Food safety: Steaks and roasting cuts of beef or lamb can be served pink: about 60°C in the centre is medium-rare, 70°C is well done."]},{"id":"5805f2b1-531d-46df-b62f-5dcc1190af5d","notes":["This soup freezes beautifully and tastes even better the next day. Make a double batch on Sunday and freeze in portions for busy weeknights.","If you can't find egusi, you can substitute with equal parts ground almonds and sesame seeds, though the texture won't be quite the same.","Food safety: Steaks and roasting cuts of beef or lamb can be served pink: about 60°C in the centre is medium-rare, 70°C is well done."]},{"id":"a7757b8c-49d7-4d6b-9355-d0c29902619a","notes":["Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"87d39f8d-6fe1-4c45-8709-46daa2413db7","notes":["Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"7b31ff4e-ba6a-483d-91db-a2cc22ae5d84","notes":["Larb is best served warm or at room temperature. If you can't find toasted rice powder at the markets, toast and grind white rice yourself, it only takes five minutes.","The heat is important: adjust the chillies to your taste, but the dish should have a good kick. Nok doesn't hold back.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"c2f3f553-eef5-4589-a0b1-9b9803ab461f","notes":["Make the filling the day before and refrigerate; assemble and bake just before serving.","Works just as well in the slow cooker: combine all filling ingredients, cook on low for 6–8 hours, then proceed from step 4.","Food safety: Slow-cooked meat is safe once it is tender and the centre has reached at least 75°C."]},{"id":"ecf9901e-a4f3-450b-b111-b11b86563e95","notes":["This stew improves the next day when the flavours have had time to meld. Cool quickly and refrigerate for up to 3 days, or freeze for up to 3 months.","If you don't have wine, use an extra 250 ml beef stock instead.","For camp oven: brown the meat and veg in a big Dutch oven, then pop it straight into the coals for 3 to 4 hours.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"5eed98de-b048-4937-b426-8268ac3eb3a0","notes":["Make the carnitas up to 2 days ahead; reheat gently in a low oven or on the stove with a splash of water.","The reserved pork cooking liquid is liquid gold, use it to warm the carnitas or sip as a broth.","No corn tortillas? Flour tortillas work fine, but corn is the real deal.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part."]},{"id":"9c2b6448-8db9-4e74-a792-5f9fff87ed52","notes":["Make the curry paste up to a day ahead and keep it covered in the fridge. Fresh fish is best for this curry, ask your fishmonger what looks good on the day.","Use whatever white fish is fresh and local: snapper, flathead or barramundi all work well.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."]},{"id":"25682751-ac2c-4e12-9c34-87058e392a49","notes":["Make a bigger batch and cool the broth quickly; it freezes well for up to 3 months.","If you can't find good pork bones, use a ham hock or extra corned beef – the broth won't be quite as rich but it'll still be aroha kai.","Fresh watercress is key. If you can't get it, try silverbeet (spinach) or spring greens – add them the same way, just wilted.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"c50877a2-b146-4f2f-9f17-8c91f29ed496","notes":["Yassa is even better the next day. Cool quickly, cover and refrigerate for up to 3 days, or freeze for up to 3 months. Reheat gently on the stove.","Use thighs and drumsticks rather than breast, they stay moist and the skin crisps beautifully in the pan.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"25ec600a-90c1-4302-a69d-763241774fc1","notes":["The dough can be made up to 8 hours ahead and kept covered at room temperature. If it dries out, spritz lightly with water.","The filling can be made several hours ahead and refrigerated. Assemble the dumplings no more than 1 hour before steaming.","If you don't have a bamboo steamer, use a metal steamer rack or even a heatproof plate set on chopsticks above the simmering water, the key is gentle, steady steam.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"f939bd18-4da9-4a1d-a0d8-9225da53bc26","notes":["Make the dosa batter 1 to 2 days ahead and keep it in the fridge. It ferments and becomes more flavourful. Use it straight from the fridge, don't bring it to room temperature.","If the batter is too thick, thin it with a splash of water. It should pour easily but still coat the pan.","The potato can be boiled and diced an hour ahead."]},{"id":"d6c72295-7eae-4632-b708-b47b82a47ed9","notes":["This keeps well in the fridge for 2 days and freezes beautifully for up to 3 months. Reheat gently on the stove.","Ayesha's tip: Don't rush the tomato-breaking-down stage. That's where the flavour sits. If you like more heat, add a pinch of dried chilli flakes with the ground spices or more green chilli.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"c43efa04-2bda-428d-b292-05ec9c97683a","notes":["Use the freshest sashimi-grade tuna available. If you're unsure about the source, ask your fishmonger.","Coconut vinegar adds a more authentic Visayan flavour, but white vinegar works well too.","Eat the same day you make it – the acid cures the fish but doesn't preserve it like cooking does.","The fish here is eaten raw or only cured in citrus. Buy sashimi-grade fish from a fishmonger, keep it cold and eat it the same day; skip it for anyone pregnant, very young, elderly or with low immunity.","Food safety: The seafood here is served raw or cured, so buy sashimi-grade, keep it cold and eat it the day it is made; for anyone who should avoid raw fish, cook it until opaque. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"61660d26-a814-4c2d-ac22-cc41b999ffc2","notes":["The laksa paste can be made the day before and kept in an airtight container in the fridge.","Use the freshest mackerel you can find, the quality makes all the difference. If fresh mackerel is hard to find, try Spanish mackerel or another firm white fish.","The broth can be made ahead and reheated gently; cook the noodles fresh just before serving.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"bf204f98-d8ef-433c-8661-0d1e4dc2b481","notes":["Make this the day before and reheat gently, the flavour deepens overnight.","Freeze the sauce in portions for up to three months.","Serve over fresh pasta, pappardelle, or rigatoni, with a scatter of fresh basil and parmesan.","Cook pork until it reaches at least 70°C in the thickest part (75°C for mince and sausages).","Food safety: Cook whole cuts of beef, lamb and pork to at least 63°C in the thickest part, then rest for 3 minutes; cook pork and any rolled or stuffed cuts until the juices run clear."]},{"id":"c712f7d0-9d23-4eb0-99d9-82d493fe43b7","notes":["Borscht keeps well in the fridge for up to 4 days, and the flavour deepens each day. Reheat gently on the stove.","For a vegetarian version, skip the beef and use vegetable stock instead; the soup will need only 45 minutes on the stove after the vegetables go in.","Some cooks add 200 g cooked kidney beans with the potatoes for a heartier soup.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"a7013c30-d19d-4d05-a994-34aa4054b037","notes":["Bredie improves overnight as the spices deepen. Make it the day before and gently reheat it.","If you can't find dried apricots, use dried peaches or a combination of apricots and raisins.","Traditionally made with mutton; lamb shoulder works beautifully and cooks a little faster.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"dacfb7da-4ddc-4f12-9a2f-2c50ad93cb31","notes":["Make the potato mixture just before frying, it will start to weep if left to sit. If you must wait, drain and squeeze it again before cooking.","The crème fraîche topping can be made up to a day ahead and kept refrigerated."]},{"id":"3a1ea6f4-9ee6-42c5-bcf5-d87e47b9415c","notes":["This adobo tastes even better the next day, anak. Keep it in the fridge and warm it gently before serving.","If you don't have calamansi, use fresh lime juice or good white vinegar, both work beautifully.","Corazon always serves this with plain steamed white rice and a simple green vegetable.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"13100f29-7e29-44e3-b2b3-f2b7d0cb923d","notes":["Cold, leftover rice works best, warm rice gets mushy. Spread it on a tray and refrigerate for at least 2 hours.","The key to those crispy golden bits (cơm cháy) is letting the rice sit untouched on the hot pan for 30 seconds at a time before stirring.","For extra flavour, add a splash of soy sauce or a drizzle of chilli oil after cooking.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through. Cool any leftover rice quickly and refrigerate it within an hour; reheat it until steaming hot."]},{"id":"fd25a6df-83e0-479a-a9ce-a6bb1a8e326b","notes":["Nduja keeps well in the fridge for weeks once opened. Wrap it tightly or keep it in an airtight container.","If you can't find nduja at your local deli, check the Italian section at a good supermarket or order it online, it's worth the hunt."]},{"id":"28ef8146-ab36-402b-91ee-fadd0dca65c9","notes":["Make a big batch of sambal matah and keep it in the fridge for up to three days, it's brilliant on rice, eggs, or any leftover protein.","If you can't find fresh lime, use lemon or bottled lime juice, but fresh is so much better.","Tempeh keeps in the fridge for ages; fry it whenever you need a quick, protein-packed lunch."]},{"id":"c023802c-efe9-4a60-8272-855837786cac","notes":["The key is drying the tofu well before frying, squeeze it gently between clean cloths to release as much water as you can.","Atsuage is best eaten straight away, while the outside is still crackling and the inside soft."]},{"id":"ffcbde4c-6199-40c3-998c-77331d4685d5","notes":["Salt cod must be soaked for 24 hours before cooking. Start the night before.","Choricero peppers are essential, they give the sauce its deep colour and flavour. Find them in Spanish delis or good supermarkets; regular dried peppers work but won't taste the same.","This dish is best made in a heavy earthenware cazuela, but any heavy-bottomed pan works fine."]},{"id":"0d179c07-2625-4181-adf1-641f44950667","notes":["Make the quenelle mixture up to 2 hours ahead and chill. The sauce can be made while the quenelles poach.","If pike isn't available, perch, barramundi or any firm white fish works well, this is a weeknight dinner, not a pilgrimage.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."]},{"id":"2686f275-a762-49c4-b6a2-798d2dd5cd17","notes":["Leftovers keep in the fridge for 3 days. Reheat gently on the stove, adding a splash of water if it's thickened too much.","Use rotisserie chicken, or shredded leftover roast chicken. If you don't have cooked chicken, add 300 g raw chicken breast to the pot with the stock and cook for 12 minutes before adding lentils.","Swap red lentils for brown lentils if you prefer a firmer texture; they'll need the full 25 minutes to cook.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."]},{"id":"795570fd-0229-4591-a731-1b8a867fb2ad","notes":["Make the sauce while the pork cooks: mix mirin, soy sauce and water in a small bowl and you're ready to go.","No fresh pork cutlets? Thin chicken breast works just as well, pound it to 5 mm and it'll cook in the same time.","Leftover rice from lunch? This dish is your friend. Just make sure it's still warm when you serve.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system. Cool any leftover rice quickly and refrigerate it within an hour; reheat it until steaming hot."]},{"id":"2d1bc7d0-5a83-4658-9281-fc64086ba898","notes":["Make the braised pork up to 2 days ahead. It keeps in the fridge in its sauce and tastes even better reheated.","The eggs are best cooked just before serving, but you can boil them ahead and reheat gently in hot water.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"5da9a85e-7457-4f20-904a-7d29bbb990da","notes":["The filling must cool completely before adding the pastry, or it'll go soggy. Make it the day before if you like.","You can freeze the uncooked pie for up to a month. Bake straight from frozen, adding 10–15 minutes to the cooking time.","Swaps: button mushrooms work fine if you can't get swiss browns, or use a mix. Crème fraîche instead of cream if that's what you've got.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"ab6e2520-83b7-4243-a9da-a272f9c6604e","notes":["Use well-aged kimchi for the deepest flavour; old kimchi that's been fermenting for months is better than fresh.","Don't skip the skimming step, it keeps the braise clean and clear.","Leftover kimchi-jjim reheats gently on the stove with a splash of water.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part."]},{"id":"da9abee3-43d7-41c8-a720-1950bff66040","notes":["Make it the day before and reheat gently, the flavours deepen. Store in an airtight container in the fridge for up to 4 days.","Serve with couscous or warm flatbread to soak up the sauce.","If you can't find saffron, add a pinch of turmeric instead for warmth and colour.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"ac3ce7b1-2d8e-450b-a10d-83e48aa64593","notes":["Make the sambal ahead and reheat it just before frying the ikan bilis, the anchovies need to go in hot to stay crispy.","If the ikan bilis are very small, you don't need to clean them; just rinse and fry whole.","For less heat, remove some of the chilli seeds before pounding."]},{"id":"6f50339e-977c-4958-8a0d-a40446c6027d","notes":["Dough can be made ahead and refrigerated for up to 24 hours. Bring to room temperature before rolling.","Cooked varenyky freeze well for up to 1 month. Reheat by boiling briefly in salted water until they float again, or pan-fry with the caramelised onions.","The filling is traditional, but varenyky are also delicious filled with fried cabbage and mushrooms, or with cherries and sugar for dessert.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"12b85923-7966-45c5-b4de-c6b257271707","notes":["Light soup keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days and freezes well for a month. Just reheat gently on the stove.","If you can't find groundnut paste, smooth peanut butter works just fine.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."]},{"id":"a0a5d421-deba-441d-a147-747a440f4127","notes":["Make the filling up to 2 hours ahead and chill. Roll the börek just before frying.","If yufka dries out, cover unused sheets with a damp tea towel.","Leftover börek can be reheated in a 180°C oven for 5 minutes to crisp up again.","Cook mince and sausages until they reach 75°C in the centre, with no pink left.","Food safety: Cook mince, sausages, meatballs and liver right through to 75°C, with no pink in the centre."]},{"id":"45f37739-4e11-446a-9dcd-7bb94bc279b6","notes":["The laksa paste is the backbone, a good quality one saves time. If you can't find tamarind paste, soak a ball of tamarind pulp in hot water for 10 minutes, then strain.","Make the broth first and it'll wait for you while you fry the tofu and fish cakes. The noodles should be cooked fresh just before serving.","Some people add prawns to this laksa. If you'd like them, toss in 200 g of cooked prawns if that's how you like it."]},{"id":"db7d5514-e686-4d29-9c68-c80cb3b94220","notes":["Makes a generous batch. Keeps in the fridge for up to 4 days or freezes well for a month.","If you can't find guajillo or ancho chillies, use 3–4 dried red chillies of any kind, the technique is what matters."]},{"id":"7c9a7135-a8bd-474b-867e-a63e0de51d28","notes":["If you prefer a thinner soup, add more stock or water before blending.","Make the crispy onion topping ahead and store in an airtight container, it keeps for 3 days and turns any soup into something special.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."]},{"id":"903c37ff-8bb0-42e8-ac5f-0c7231417925","notes":["The galette batter can be made up to 4 hours ahead and kept covered in the fridge. Bring to room temperature for 10 minutes before cooking.","Clean mussels well under cold running water, pulling away any beard. Discard any that are open and won't close when tapped.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through. Throw away any mussels or clams whose shells stay closed after cooking. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"1143f50b-ffdd-4a24-ac8b-0d382470fb07","notes":["Swap the corned beef for tinned tuna or cooked chicken if that's what you've got.","Serve over rice or with fresh bread to soak up the sauce.","Any leftover veg works here, carrots, green beans, capsicum, whatever's in the crisper."]},{"id":"bc274cd3-d7d9-4f42-ab9c-aa7ec10ba49a","notes":["The dough can be made up to 4 hours ahead, wrapped and refrigerated. Let it come to room temperature before stretching.","The broth keeps for 3 days in the fridge and tastes even better the next day.","If you're nervous about pulling noodles, you can roll the final dough thin and cut it into thick strands instead, it won't be quite the same, but it'll still be good.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"0825b0b4-4cb3-4e04-b487-57cbde067892","notes":["The ragù freezes beautifully for up to three months. Make a double batch when you have time.","Fresh tagliatelle is essential here. If you can't find it, use dried pappardelle, cook it to packet instructions instead.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"11dab63b-78c0-46ee-a2c2-c7abf838c67f","notes":["This curry tastes even better the next day. Reheat gently on the stove or in the microwave until warm through.","Use any firm white fish you like, snapper, barramundi, bream or cod all work well. Avoid delicate fish that will fall apart.","Serve with steamed rice, appam, or flatbread to soak up the coconut broth.","Food safety: Cook fish until it turns opaque and flakes easily, about 63°C in the thickest part."]},{"id":"9105b802-80fa-43cc-828c-f147d235a38b","notes":["Pilau tastes even better the next day. Reheat gently with a splash of water.","If beef chuck is hard to find, use beef shin or any tough cut that braises well.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."]},{"id":"3f3b1c25-e662-4f68-8ee9-942c2bea7456","notes":["If you can't find fresh holy basil, Thai basil works fine, use the same amount. Regular basil will change the flavour but won't ruin it.","Prep everything before you start cooking: mince the garlic, slice the chillies, tear the basil, measure the sauce. Once the wok hits temperature, there's no time to stop.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"99841bff-a1c6-4799-9739-34bba4852be3","notes":["Make the filling while the onion cooks, so everything comes together quickly.","Keep your hands wet when rolling, it stops the meat from sticking and makes shaping easier.","These freeze well raw; just add a couple of extra minutes to the cooking time straight from the freezer.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"8dad6e5e-6e0b-460e-a64c-eb64418a883f","notes":["Tastes just as good at room temperature, perfect for setting out on the table while you cook other things.","You can add a pinch of smoked paprika or cumin if you want a little more flavour, but honestly, the simplicity is the point."]},{"id":"d5bc89ff-5752-4927-9193-246450c7b6c0","notes":["If you can't find fresh crab, use 400 g tinned crab or frozen crab meat, thawed.","The noodle batter can be made up to 2 hours ahead and refrigerated; let it come to room temperature before using.","A bánh canh press makes thick, even noodles, but a large-holed colander works just as well.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."]},{"id":"60c65a28-57d4-4f40-a002-cc976680a473","notes":["Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"62da20d0-4993-4335-8d08-dcd4c146cb7a","notes":["Use bone-in chicken thighs if you prefer, they stay juicier. Cut around the bone and into cubes.","Presoaking wooden skewers for 30 minutes prevents burning, but metal skewers work best and cook faster.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."]},{"id":"a926503a-6e11-45b1-912b-b49cb551f5dc","notes":["The chickpea curry can be made ahead and reheated gently; make the bread fresh just before serving for the best texture.","If your kitchen scales aren't handy, the bread dough works with about 2 cups flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 1 tbsp oil, and ¾ cup warm water.","Doubles are best eaten fresh and hot, the contrast between the warm spiced chickpea and crispy-soft bread is half the magic."]},{"id":"1bab1394-d507-45b9-a23d-6fddf5ca6041","notes":["The syrup can be made an hour ahead and kept warm in a low oven or over a very low heat. It must be hot when you dip the koeksisters.","Make sure your oil is at the right temperature, if it's not hot enough, the koeksisters will be greasy instead of crisp.","Store cooled koeksisters in an airtight container for up to 2 days, though they're best fresh and warm.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"4fe7221e-0b17-415c-95a4-4a128416a556","notes":["The pickled beetroot can be made a day ahead and keeps in the fridge for up to a week.","If you can't find waxy potatoes, use any small firm potatoes and cut them in half.","Frikadeller freeze well uncooked; shape and refrigerate on a tray for 30 minutes, then freeze in a bag for up to three months.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."]},{"id":"cb1d0e4b-b92d-4a82-bfdc-7b03ed618a7a","notes":["Make ahead: Mix the dough and shape the arepas up to 2 hours before cooking, then refrigerate, bring to room temperature before cooking.","Swap: If you can't find queso de mano, fresh mozzarella or even mild cheddar works, though it won't be quite the same.","You can cook arepas on a griddle, heavy-bottomed frying pan, or a cast-iron frying pan, whatever you have. The sound and smell tell you more than the time does."]},{"id":"7bd16276-d32b-4b2c-924c-8332fb44d4ba","notes":["Cook the rice in advance and cool quickly; reheat gently before serving if you like. You can marinate the tofu overnight.","Fried shallots keep in an airtight container for a week.","The marinade ingredients are flexible, add a pinch of chilli flakes or a splash of rice vinegar if you like."]},{"id":"3bc678bc-9e47-4c66-a8a7-9d37469b7111","notes":["The cucumber salad can be made up to 2 hours ahead and kept in the fridge. Drain before serving.","Use the best-quality pâté and bacon you can find, this sandwich lives or dies by those two things.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear.","Cook pork until it reaches at least 70°C in the thickest part (75°C for mince and sausages)."]},{"id":"ffc72f75-a873-4746-b046-207b26a72ca1","notes":["The soup thickens considerably as it cools. Reheat gently with a little water if needed.","Make it a day ahead, the flavour improves overnight. Store in the fridge for up to 4 days."]}]$q$::jsonb)), up as (update public.published_recipes p set recipe = jsonb_set(p.recipe, '{notes}', d.r->'notes') from d where p.id=(d.r->>'id')::uuid returning p.id) select (select count(*) from d) expected, (select count(*) from up) updated;